 "name": "Post-Mining Forests – Perceived Characteristics & Walk Attractiveness",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Braun Kohlová, Nepožitková & Melichar (2021), \"How Do Observable Characteristics of Post-Mining Forests Affect Their Attractiveness for Recreation?\", Land 10:910. Participants first look at an overview of forest photographs, then see six forests in a random order: five restored forests on coal-mine spoil heaps near Sokolov, Czech Republic (re-planted alder and pine at 35 years; spontaneous succession at 15, 35 and 55 years) and one ordinary 75-year-old spruce forest. In Part 1 they rate each forest on five bipolar items (unnatural–natural, not maintained–maintained, inaccessible–accessible, not familiar–familiar, dangerous–safe; -2..2). In Part 2, in the same order, they rate \"How tempting would it be for you to walk on a forest way in this forest for one hour?\" (-2 not at all … 2 very tempting). In the original study (869 Czech adults), post-mining forests were less attractive than the spruce forest mainly because they looked less accessible, less maintained and less safe.\n\nPhotographs and questionnaire wording: Braun Kohlová et al. (2021), Figure 1 and Appendix A, CC BY 4.0. Used with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) each forest is shown with one photograph (the paper's Figure 1) instead of a quartet of four, because only one photo per forest is published; (2) the overview uses the 8 overview photos printed in Appendix A; (3) English only (original Czech); (4) the discrete choice experiment in Part 3 (reported elsewhere) is left out; (5) demographics are limited to gender, age and education, and a consent checkbox was added.",
